{"name":"Moonlight Blue","hex":"#567090","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"moonlight blue","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A grayish shade of blue."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moonlight_blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Moonlight","blocks":[{"text":"Moonlight is light from the surface of the Moon, consisting mostly of reflected sunlight, and some earthlight."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Earthshine_Moon.jpg/330px-Earthshine_Moon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moonlight","wikidata":"Q1058800"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Tonsvatnet%2C_t%C3%A5ke_og_m%C3%A5ne.JPG/500px-Tonsvatnet%2C_t%C3%A5ke_og_m%C3%A5ne.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Tonsvatnet%2C_t%C3%A5ke_og_m%C3%A5ne.JPG/1280px-Tonsvatnet%2C_t%C3%A5ke_og_m%C3%A5ne.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm